// expect: 2
// A pipe into `slice` whose bounds a guard proves is the cut beneath the
// check, so `main` keeps its `!Trap` promise: nothing it calls can stop with
// a trap. The pinned compiler raised no claim at the stage, so the stage
// charged the checked cut's `Trap` and `!Trap` was refused.
fn first_two(xs) = if len(xs) >= 2 { xs |> slice(??, 0, 2) } else { [] }

fn main() with !Trap = len(first_two([1, 2, 3]))
